{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>He has defiled the sanctuary of YHWH</b> – Scripture mentions this punishment of excision (כָּרֵת) in general terms and says, \"he has defiled the Tabernacle of YHWH\" (above, v. 13), because it had already warned concerning the sanctuary against impurity, as it says: \"she shall touch no hallowed thing, nor come into the sanctuary\" (Leviticus 12:4). For there it mentioned the lighter impurity, that of a woman who has given birth during the days of her purification, which is in her childbearing, and the same applies to all those who are impure. And it had already also mentioned immersion: \"and if she does not wash her clothes and bathe her flesh, she shall bear her iniquity\" (ibid. 15:30 [n: Ramban cites the verse by its content; the reference as printed reads as Leviticus 17:16, which appears to be an error]), meaning that she bears her iniquity for transgressing that from which she was warned. Therefore it says here concerning the impurity of the dead: a person who touches a corpse and does not purify himself, even though he washes his garments and bathes in water, is considered to have defiled the Tabernacle as though he had not immersed at all. And there was no need to explain anything other than that the sprinkling (חִטּוּי) is a requisite for his purification. And this is the meaning of \"his impurity is still upon him\" (v. 13) — that is, even though he immersed like the rest of those who purify themselves, he is still impure because the waters of the purification offering (מֵי נִדָּה) were not sprinkled upon him. And it is possible that it alludes to the word אִישׁ (\"a man\"), its meaning being: any man who touches a corpse and does not purify himself — who defiles the Tabernacle of YHWH — shall be cut off. And its parallel is found with the omission of a שׁ or the word אֲשֶׁר in many places: \"unto all who come for your might\" (Psalms 71:18 [n: the printed text reads תהלים עא יח; the quoted phrase lacks the expected שׁ/אֲשֶׁר before the relative]), \"and all who were in his hand he gave into his hand\" (Genesis 39:4 [n: similarly lacking the relative particle]), \"the way they should walk in it\" (Exodus 18:20), and many more like these. And the meaning of <b>\"for he has defiled the sanctuary of YHWH\"</b> (Numbers 19:20) — is: when he has defiled the sanctuary of YHWH, like \"if a bird's nest chance to be before you\" (Deuteronomy 22:6) and its many parallels. And Scripture mentions excision twice. According to our Sages (Shevuot 16a/b [n: the citation שבועות ט״ו refers to the Talmudic discussion of the two forms of excision in this passage]), this is to hold him liable for [defiling] the Tabernacle, the Tent of Meeting, and the Sanctuary, the eternal Temple. And it is possible according to the plain sense that \"the sanctuary of YHWH\" refers to sacrificial offerings (קָדְשֵׁי הַקָּרְבָּן), for it had already imposed excision upon one who eats the flesh of the sacrifice of peace offerings belonging to YHWH while his impurity is upon him (Leviticus 7:20). And here it imposes liability further upon one who does not purify himself on the third day and on the seventh day even though he immersed; and the meaning of \"the sanctuary of YHWH\" would be \"the holy things of YHWH,\" as in \"from its holy things\" (Numbers 18:29). Thus the excision in this pericope applies to [defiling] both the sanctuary and its holy things. And the correct interpretation in my eyes according to the plain sense is that the first excision applies to one who touches a corpse (here, v. 13), as it says, \"anyone who touches a corpse, the person of a dead body,\" while the second applies also to those who are tented (מַאֲהִילִים) over a corpse, who did not touch a bone or a grave. For the meaning of \"and a man who becomes impure and does not purify himself\" (Numbers 19:20) is: and a man who becomes impure by any of these [cases] mentioned and does not purify himself. And by way of truth, it is possible that \"the sanctuary of YHWH\" alludes to the sanctuary of the Sanctuary (מִקְדַּשׁ הַמִּקְדָּשׁ)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:07:11.830316898Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}